---
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';

const skillToolsetCode = `import pathlib

from google.adk import Agent
from google.adk.skills import load_skill_from_dir
from google.adk.tools import skill_toolset

diagnostics_skill = load_skill_from_dir(
    pathlib.Path(__file__).parent / "skills" / "manufacturing-diagnostics"
)

diagnostics_tools = skill_toolset.SkillToolset(
    skills=[diagnostics_skill],
    additional_tools=[read_alarm_history, fetch_sensor_window],
)

root_agent = Agent(
    model="gemini-2.5-flash",
    name="diagnostics_agent",
    instruction="Use the diagnostics skill before proposing maintenance.",
    tools=[diagnostics_tools],
)`;
---

<SiteLayout current="skills" title="Skills">
  <section class="section page-section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Tool form 03B</p>
      <h1>Skills package repeatable know-how.</h1>
      <p>
        A skill is more than an instruction. It can bundle procedure, examples,
        scripts, templates, and domain constraints so the agent performs a
        workflow the same way every time.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="signal-panel signal-panel--definitions" style="margin-top: 32px; margin-bottom: 48px;">
      <div>
        <article>
          <span>Use when</span>
          <strong>The work has a method.</strong>
          <p>Skills fit recurring workflows: render a document, analyze a dataset, prepare a release note, run a diagnostics playbook, or follow a company-specific review process.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span>Include</span>
          <strong>Instructions plus assets.</strong>
          <p>Put the core procedure in one readable guide. Add scripts for mechanical steps, templates for output shape, and examples that show what good execution looks like.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span>Design for</span>
          <strong>Triggering and scope.</strong>
          <p>Make it obvious when the skill should be used, what it owns, and when it should stop. The best skills reduce ambiguity without hiding the work.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-head compact-head" style="margin-top: 40px;">
      <p class="eyebrow">The protocol</p>
      <h2>How a skill is loaded and invoked.</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="mini-diagram react-diagram" aria-label="Skill execution flow diagram" style="margin-bottom: 32px;">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 640 160" role="img">
        <defs>
          <marker id="react-arrow" markerHeight="8" markerWidth="8" orient="auto" refX="7" refY="4">
            <path d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 Z"></path>
          </marker>
        </defs>
        <g class="react-links">
          <path d="M 124 56 H 180"></path>
          <path d="M 300 56 H 356"></path>
          <path d="M 476 56 H 532"></path>
        </g>
        <g class="react-node-svg">
          <rect x="24" y="34" width="100" height="44" rx="22"></rect>
          <text x="74" y="60">Load</text>
        </g>
        <g class="react-node-svg is-planner">
          <rect x="180" y="34" width="120" height="44" rx="22"></rect>
          <text x="240" y="60">Trigger</text>
        </g>
        <g class="react-node-svg">
          <rect x="356" y="34" width="120" height="44" rx="22"></rect>
          <text x="416" y="60">Execute</text>
        </g>
        <g class="react-node-svg is-done">
          <rect x="532" y="34" width="100" height="44" rx="22"></rect>
          <text x="582" y="60">Return</text>
        </g>
        <text class="loop-caption" x="320" y="116">from manifest to bundled multi-step execution</text>
      </svg>
    </div>

    <ul class="timeline-list">
      <li>
        <span class="timeline-number">01</span>
        <div>
          <strong>Load</strong>
          <p>The runtime reads the skill directory: a manifest (name, description, triggering hints), a primary instruction file, and any bundled scripts, templates, or examples. The skill becomes a discoverable capability, not a hardcoded prompt.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="timeline-number">02</span>
        <div>
          <strong>Trigger</strong>
          <p>The model decides the skill applies. This is the step that's genuinely different from function tools: the manifest's description does the matching, so the agent reaches for the skill when the situation looks like the situations it's been written for.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="timeline-number">03</span>
        <div>
          <strong>Execute</strong>
          <p>The runtime hands the skill's instructions back to the model, with the bundled scripts and templates available as supporting context. The model follows the packaged procedure step by step — including any calls into ordinary function tools the skill declares as helpers.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="timeline-number">04</span>
        <div>
          <strong>Return</strong>
          <p>The skill's output flows back into the session as a normal tool response. From the orchestration loop's perspective, the skill was a single capability invocation; from inside, it was a multi-step procedure with its own structure.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p class="mechanism-note">
      Function tools enforce <em>contracts</em>: a typed call, a typed result. Skills
      enforce <em>method</em>: the same procedure, executed the same way each time.
      The skill's manifest is what makes triggering reliable — vague descriptions
      produce vague matching.
    </p>

    <figure class="code-card code-card--standalone">
      <figcaption>
        <span class="lang-pill">python</span>
        <span class="code-filename">google_adk_skill_toolset.py</span>
        <a href="https://adk.dev/skills/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Google ADK docs</a>
      </figcaption>
      <Code code={skillToolsetCode} lang="python" theme="github-light" />
    </figure>

    <div class="section-head compact-head" style="margin-top: 48px;">
      <p class="eyebrow">Further reading</p>
      <h2>Official skills documentation.</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="refs-grid">
      <a class="refs-block" href="https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/agents-and-tools/agent-skills/overview" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
        <span>Concept</span>
        <h3>Anthropic Agent Skills</h3>
        <p>
          The packaged-know-how pattern in its original form: instructions,
          scripts, templates, and metadata that Claude can discover and
          invoke. Useful for understanding the shape and intent before
          looking at any one framework's implementation.
        </p>
        <div class="refs-link">docs.claude.com/agent-skills <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></div>
      </a>
      <a class="refs-block" href="https://adk.dev/skills/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
        <span>Implementation</span>
        <h3>Google ADK Skills</h3>
        <p>
          ADK's take: <code>load_skill_from_dir</code> plus
          <code>SkillToolset</code>, with the skill packaged as a directory
          on disk and exposed to the agent through the standard tools
          interface. This is what the code sample above uses.
        </p>
        <div class="refs-link">adk.dev/skills <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></div>
      </a>
      <a class="refs-block" href="https://agentskills.io/home" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
        <span>Open Standard</span>
        <h3>Agent Skills</h3>
        <p>
          A lightweight, open standard designed to extend AI agents through 
          structured workflows and progressive disclosure. It packages instructions, 
          scripts, and reference material into portable, version-controlled folders 
          for cross-product reuse.
        </p>
        <div class="refs-link">agentskills.io/home <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></div>
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
